\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\title{Steady One-Dimensional Flow --- Tutorial Notes}
\author{Thermofluids III, Problem Set 4}
\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Setup}
We consider a rigid control volume with one inlet and one outlet. The
flow is steady, so every inventory derivative vanishes and each balance
reduces to an algebraic relation between the two stations. Station 1 is
the inlet, station 2 the outlet, and all specific quantities are per
unit mass.

Before writing any balances, fix the sign convention: heat transfer
into the control volume is positive, shaft work done by the fluid is
positive, and velocities are taken positive from inlet to outlet. Most
sign errors in this problem set trace back to mixing conventions
halfway through a calculation.

\section{Governing relations}
For steady one-dimensional flow through a control volume, mass
conservation reads
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:continuity}
\dot{m} = \rho_1 A_1 V_1 = \rho_2 A_2 V_2
\end{equation}
and the momentum balance between the inlet and outlet stations gives
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:momentum}
p_1 A_1 - p_2 A_2 = \dot{m} \left( V_2 - V_1 \right)
\end{equation}
while the steady-flow energy equation, including heat transfer and
shaft work, is
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:energy}
\dot{Q} - \dot{W}_s = \dot{m} \left( h_2 - h_1 + \frac{V_2^{2} - V_1^{2}}{2} \right)
\end{equation}
As equation (3) shows, the shaft work term couples directly to the
enthalpy rise across the machine, which is why turbines and compressors
are always analysed with the energy equation rather than the momentum
equation alone.

\section{Which balance to use}
Continuity is almost never the binding constraint; it merely fixes the
mass flux once the geometry and one state are known. The momentum
equation earns its keep when pressure forces dominate, as in nozzles and
diffusers with negligible heat transfer. For anything with a shaft or a
heating coil, go straight to the energy equation and treat the momentum
balance as a consistency check.

A common exam mistake is to apply Bernoulli's equation across a pump or
turbine. Bernoulli assumes no shaft work, so it is silently invalid
across any machine; the energy equation above is the correct
generalisation and reduces to Bernoulli only when both the work and the
heat terms vanish.

\section{Worked problem}
Air enters an adiabatic compressor at a low velocity and leaves at a
higher pressure with a negligible change in kinetic energy. With the
kinetic term dropped, the energy equation collapses to a balance
between shaft work input and enthalpy rise, and the exit temperature
follows directly from the ideal-gas relation for enthalpy.

\section{Further reading}
The derivation of the steady-flow energy equation from the transient
first law is given in the course textbook, chapter 5; the same chapter
covers the lost-work decomposition that we skipped here.

\end{document}

